A||||10||RobH||Maximum bore on V6||||5||
Z||000000||RobH||05-30-2000||12:14 AM||||What is the maximum you can bore a 2.8 V6?  I am seriously thinking about rebuilding my engine, have low compression, and would have to bore to 0.040" over cause the #2 cylinder is 'wore slap out' 0.032" over ( it is at 3.532" ).  Anyone have any problems boring this much over?<P>The engine appears to never have been rebuilt.<P>Thanks...<BR>Rob||||reg||5
Z||000001||80's BOY||05-30-2000||11:23 AM||||when i got my 2.8, the #6 rod had broke off, wrapped around, caught the crank, broke off a lobe which flew through the oil pan, and the remainder pounded out the #6 cyl. with large cuts to the inside. my machinist said to take a chance and go 0.060 over instead of sleeving it. then we put a 3.1 kit in, and all the v8 guys said it will run HOT HOT HOT and i'll never get it tuned. WRONG!! i've had great luck with mine, and it purrs like a kitten. i can say up to 0.060 is very safe, and my temp never goes above 180 in south texas thanks to the HYPERTECH fan temp switch. the cyl. walls aren't as thin as you think they are, dude. go for it!!!||||reg||6
Z||000002||batboy||05-30-2000||12:20 PM||||If the casting cores are dead on, 0.060" over bore should be considered maximum. Some blocks have a slight core shift which means the wall might get a little thin on one side of the cylinder at 0.060". However, I've never heard of a 2.8 V6 having a problem being bored out to 0.040". I say go for it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||N_Man||05-30-2000||12:40 PM||||is .060 over worth risking it?  How likly is the cylinder wall going to crack?<P>My machinist just called saying .060 or a new, used, block for $100 CDN. lots of money and work is going to this project and I want my engine to be reliable.<BR>PLEASE HELP||||reg||1
Z||000004||80's BOY||05-30-2000||01:52 PM||||i had over $500 in the machine work alone, counting the heads also. if you're really cautious, get it line bored like i did. just to check the overall stability and quality of the block. then go for the overbore. you can magnaflux everything before and after to be really careful. i only went 0.060 because i had to, i don't think i would have asked for it. it is a very reliable engine. my rpm's are well into the 6-9 grand range from the stroke. i guess this is normal. i've pushed it really,really hard and it seems to love the challenge.||||reg||14
Z||000005||batboy||05-30-2000||02:16 PM||||Machine shops have a way to check thickness. Maybe ultrasound(?) or something like that. I know racers use this method to sort through several blocks to find the best ones. In most cases, especially if this no more than a mild performance street engine, 0.060" will work ok, it's just getting close to pushing the limit, that's what I wanted to warn you about.||||reg||1
Z||000006||N_Man||05-30-2000||04:28 PM||||Talked to the shop he said he magnafluxed the block and it was in perfect shape and I might want to run the engine a bit cooler. So I said go for it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||80's BOY||05-30-2000||04:46 PM||||so does that mean 0.060???? yes!!!||||reg||12
Z||000008||olympic||05-30-2000||07:44 PM||||  My dad owned his own garage for 25 years and he would never install a rebuilt engine that was bore 0.060" over.  0.030" was the max he would accept.  He said he had installed a couple of 0.060" over engines when he started in business and all of them came back a couple of months later with overheating or other problems. <BR>   Think about this, what happens when your 0.060" over engine has 100, 000 miles on it and the walls are wore another 10-20 thousanths.  The walls will be paper thin.  I say spend the extra $100 and get a block that can be rebuilt and bored only 0.030" over.||||reg||1
Z||000009||RobH||05-30-2000||11:27 PM||||I called several shops around town and they all range in the $850-$895 for a long-block rebuild.  Does this sound consistent with the norm?||||reg||1
Z||000010||80's BOY||05-31-2000||12:03 PM||||screw it. time for a v8!!!||||reg||6
